{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V2m59M14Me9ZnauKz7kaNY","type":"tool_result","content":"160\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, starts `fffe` (D19, LE: `4e00`) or `feff` (D20, BE: `004e`), every other byte `00`, ~4 hex digits per char (prompt \"two bytes\", \"little\"/\"big\", \"first\", \"endian\", \"wide\", \"Same bar\") | hex of UTF-16: `xxd -r -p blob.txt \\| iconv -f UTF-16 -t UTF-8` or `bytes.fromhex(t).decode('utf-16')` -- both honour EITHER BOM; no BOM -> `utf-16-le`/`-be` by the NUL position; `fffe0000`/`0000feff` -> `utf-32`. Triage labels it \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) -- trust the charset |\n161\t| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n162\t| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n163\t| base64 starting `H4sI` gzip (D21) or `eN` zlib `78da` (D22; prompt \"wrapper\", \"compressed\", \"pressure\", \"squeeze\", \"inflation\", \"deflated\"); also `eJ`/`eF`/`eAE` other zlib levels, `QlpoOTFBWSZT` bz2, `/Td6WFo` xz, `KLUv/` zstd, `BCJNGA` lz4, `UEsDB` zip. Hex: `1f8b`, `78xx`, `425a68`, `fd377a585a`, `28b52ffd`, `504b0304` | `base64 -d blob.txt \\| gzip -dc` (D21); `python3 -c \"import base64,zlib,sys;print(zlib.decompress(base64.b64decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read())).decode())\" blob.txt` (D22; `zlib.decompress(b,47)` auto-detects zlib OR gzip, `-15` = headerless); `lib.decode.peel` does the stdlib ones. Triage says \"looks base64\" -- right, but the bytes are binary: decompress, don't score |\n164\t| `<~` ... `~>` around `!`-`u` printable soup (`<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGml...~>`, ~1.25 x the text; prompt \"alphabet got bigger\", \"Adobe\", \"Eighty-Five\", \"neighbors\") | Ascii85 (D23): `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True).decode())\" blob.txt`. No delimiters -> `a85decode(t)`; `0-9A-Za-z!#$%&()*+-;<=>?@^_{\\|}~` -> `b85decode`; `.-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$#` -> Z85; `xbtoa Begin` -> btoa. Then sniff magic (PDF-style a85 of zlib) |\n165\t| `1:N,1:i,1:c,1:e,1: ,...` -- `digits:payload,` repeated, ~4x the text, the payload's `,`/`:` framed too (`1:,,`, `1::,`) (prompt \"count\", \"comma\", \"stop\", \"string\", \"length\") | netstrings (D24): cursor parse, NEVER split on `,`: loop `m=re.compile(r'(\\d+):').match(t,i); k=int(m[1]); j=m.end(); out.append(t[j:j+k]); assert t[j+k] == ','; i=j+k+1` (D24 solver `_netstr`). Siblings: `l4:spame` bencode, `5:hello,` + type tag = tnetstring, `$5\\r\\n` RESP, hex-size lines = HTTP chunked, `N:c` with N > len = index or run count |\n166\t| `U+004E U+0069 U+0063 ...` -- only `U+` + 4 hex tokens, one space apart, ~7 B per char (prompt \"point\", \"code point\", \"every character\") | code points (D25): `python3 -c \"import re,sys;print(''.join(chr(int(h,16)) for h in re.findall(r'[Uu]\\+([0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8})',open(sys.argv[1]).read())))\" blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.uplus_dec` (also `\\U0001F600`, `%u`, `0x`). Names (`LATIN CAPITAL LETTER N`) -> `unicodedata.lookup` |\n167\t| Only `+ - .` (or `+-<>[].,`), runs of one sign then `.`, ~31 B per char, no letters (prompt \"language\", \"small\", \"brain\", \"food for thought\", \"say\") | Brainfuck (D26), loop-free one-cell form: `python3 -c \"import sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read();print(''.join(chr((t[:i].count('+')-t[:i].count('-'))%256) for i,c in enumerate(t) if c=='.'))\" blob.txt`; anything with `<>[]` -> `lib.warmups_codecs.bf_dec`. NEVER run untrusted programs outside a step-capped pure-python interpreter |\n168\t| `Ook. Ook. Ook! Ook! ...` -- 2-3 word+mark tokens, ~314 B per char (prompt quotes the tokens, \"chatty\", \"monkey\", \"ape\", \"librarian\"); `Blub.`, `MoO mOo`, `pi ka pikachu`, any <= 8 repeating words with run structure | BF token dialect (D27 Ook!): `python3 -I -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.bfd_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (Ook/Blub/COW/Pikalang/Alphuck/ReverseFuck/Binaryfuck + crib-solved unknown map). Hand: tokens `re.findall(r'Ook([.?!])')`, pair from 0, `Ook! Ook.` = OUTPUT. Sanity: number of output pairs == sentence length (~108-110) |\n169\t| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\n170\tThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n171\t\n172\t## Day by day\n173\t**Days 1-13** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal, URL `%xx`, decimal entities, quoted\n174\t`\"\\u00XX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, reversed sentence, Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s. Lessons (details in\n175\tPitfalls): D1 same-named downloads overwrote each other (match by sha256); D3 a slow English scorer timed Mastery out; D4\n176\trot-N needs a margin over the unshifted text; D6 a shell `read` loop lost the unterminated last token; D7 decoding the\n177\tWHOLE Mastery zip gave another set's slug (read the member); D8 a `str.replace` patch silently no-op'd; D11 strict len%4\n178\trefused unpadded b64; D12 rot-N \"decoded\" reversed text (`69-fove-n`); D13 free lines must be assigned JOINTLY.\n179\t\n180\t**Days 14-27** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n181\t+ `fffe`; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff`; gzip in base64; zlib `78da` in base64; Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`; netstrings\n182\t`1:N,1:i,...`; `U+XXXX` code points; loop-free one-cell Brainfuck; the same program in Ook! pairs): single CORRECT in\n183\t4.5-39 s, first submit each day. Lessons (details in Pitfalls): D14 rank 3-word slugs first; D15 ROT47 needs\n184\t`register_free`; D16/D17 a carrier's 16-symbol output is a middle layer; D17 Mastery needed an early return; D18 lib's\n185\t`rail_solve` existed but the carried solver never routed to it; D19's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16` carried D20 and D21's\n186\tmagic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 unchanged (write the GENERIC rule on day N); D21 Mastery died at 60 s on an ad-hoc\n187\tsolver; D23 and D25 Mastery all-NN-wrong from a pre-decoded line (fix: `register_free(name, day, fn)` on the RAW line);\n188\tD24 a strict whole-text cursor parser sits first -- a structural codec's repeating unit (`\\d+:.,`) IS the cue. D26\n189\t(`+ - .` soup; atlas had rated BF LOW): worker wrote a step-capped `_bf` (~27 s); Mastery died at 60 s because the\n190\truntime DAYS[26] encoder (`'+'*ord(c)+'.>'`) didn't reproduce the GM's DELTA bytes. D27 (\"Ook. Ook? Ook! Someone seems\n191\tchatty.\"/\"Monkey Business\"): 33929 B, 3393 Ook pairs, only `+ - .` pairs, no `Ook?` despite the prompt; lib's prebuilt\n192\t`bfd_dec` covered it, single ~33 s; the solver's DAYS[27] Ook encoder was absolute again (Mastery solve 34.9 s of 60).\n193\tA parallel live worker swapped `Ook! Ook.` (output) / `Ook. Ook!` (input). A program blob means INTERPRET (pure python,\n194\tstep cap); when lib has a prebuilt decoder for the family, call it first and register ITS encoder.\n195\t\n196\t**Day 28: Back in Sixteen** (\"Our sixteen-fingered friend is walking backwards.\" -- REVERSED HEX).\n197\t- 220-byte `blob.txt`, one line, `[0-9a-e]` only, even length, no newline: D3's hex of the sentence, the STRING reversed\n198\t  char by char (`...6e4` at the end = `4e` \"N\" read backwards). Pun: D3's own riddle (\"sixteen fingers\" = hex) +\n199\t  \"walking backwards\" = D12 reversal; title \"Back in Sixteen\" (Back in Black) = back(wards) + sixteen. Triage again said\n200\t  \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) and its idea list said only \"hex\" -- the second clue came from the prompt verb.\n201\t- Command: `python3 -c \"import sys;print(bytes.fromhex(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip()[::-1]).decode())\" blob.txt`.\n202\t  Forward `fromhex` gives `#S\\xd2GVvveu...` and byte-reversed gives `\\xe4\\x966V\\x02w...` (nibble-swapped) -- neither is\n203\t  a slug; reverse the CHARACTERS (or byte-reverse + nibble-swap).\n204\t- Single: the harness race probe (prebuilt lib `decode_chunk` reversal + hex / tgt:free-over-strict) submitted CORRECT at\n205\t  start+8 s, first try; slug as printed. TT 5.1 s, Mastery 138 x 28 9.3 s (solve 6.2 s), both first try. The carried\n206\t  solver registered NO day-28 codec: line 28 was the LAST Mastery line, so `_positional`'s unknown-last-line path\n207\t  (`decode_chunk`: reversal then hex) decoded it. Tomorrow line 28 is a MIDDLE line -- register it (Pitfalls).\n208\t- Lesson: after a 2-day esolang run the lane went back to COMPOSING two early codecs, cued by quoting an old prompt;\n209\t  expect more remixes (reversed octal/binary/base64, rot13 over base64, ...) -- undo the outer free layer, then peel."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"93819e34-795d-46dc-ac6a-56c07da4c6a6","uuid":"9c72da1b-d554-423f-8e15-f8cfc519578d","timestamp":"2026-10-31T03:42:11.467Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/reference/techniques/warmups.md","content":"| Only `[0-9a-f]`, starts `fffe` (D19, LE: `4e00`) or `feff` (D20, BE: `004e`), every other byte `00`, ~4 hex digits per char (prompt \"two bytes\", \"little\"/\"big\", \"first\", \"endian\", \"wide\", \"Same bar\") | hex of UTF-16: `xxd -r -p blob.txt \\| iconv -f UTF-16 -t UTF-8` or `bytes.fromhex(t).decode('utf-16')` -- both honour EITHER BOM; no BOM -> `utf-16-le`/`-be` by the NUL position; `fffe0000`/`0000feff` -> `utf-32`. Triage labels it \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) -- trust the charset |\n| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n| base64 starting `H4sI` gzip (D21) or `eN` zlib `78da` (D22; prompt \"wrapper\", \"compressed\", \"pressure\", \"squeeze\", \"inflation\", \"deflated\"); also `eJ`/`eF`/`eAE` other zlib levels, `QlpoOTFBWSZT` bz2, `/Td6WFo` xz, `KLUv/` zstd, `BCJNGA` lz4, `UEsDB` zip. Hex: `1f8b`, `78xx`, `425a68`, `fd377a585a`, `28b52ffd`, `504b0304` | `base64 -d blob.txt \\| gzip -dc` (D21); `python3 -c \"import base64,zlib,sys;print(zlib.decompress(base64.b64decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read())).decode())\" blob.txt` (D22; `zlib.decompress(b,47)` auto-detects zlib OR gzip, `-15` = headerless); `lib.decode.peel` does the stdlib ones. Triage says \"looks base64\" -- right, but the bytes are binary: decompress, don't score |\n| `<~` ... `~>` around `!`-`u` printable soup (`<~:2+3L+EqaECER/!@rGml...~>`, ~1.25 x the text; prompt \"alphabet got bigger\", \"Adobe\", \"Eighty-Five\", \"neighbors\") | Ascii85 (D23): `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;print(base64.a85decode(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip(),adobe=True).decode())\" blob.txt`. No delimiters -> `a85decode(t)`; `0-9A-Za-z!#$%&()*+-;<=>?@^_{\\|}~` -> `b85decode`; `.-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$#` -> Z85; `xbtoa Begin` -> btoa. Then sniff magic (PDF-style a85 of zlib) |\n| `1:N,1:i,1:c,1:e,1: ,...` -- `digits:payload,` repeated, ~4x the text, the payload's `,`/`:` framed too (`1:,,`, `1::,`) (prompt \"count\", \"comma\", \"stop\", \"string\", \"length\") | netstrings (D24): cursor parse, NEVER split on `,`: loop `m=re.compile(r'(\\d+):').match(t,i); k=int(m[1]); j=m.end(); out.append(t[j:j+k]); assert t[j+k] == ','; i=j+k+1` (D24 solver `_netstr`). Siblings: `l4:spame` bencode, `5:hello,` + type tag = tnetstring, `$5\\r\\n` RESP, hex-size lines = HTTP chunked, `N:c` with N > len = index or run count |\n| `U+004E U+0069 U+0063 ...` -- only `U+` + 4 hex tokens, one space apart, ~7 B per char (prompt \"point\", \"code point\", \"every character\") | code points (D25): `python3 -c \"import re,sys;print(''.join(chr(int(h,16)) for h in re.findall(r'[Uu]\\+([0-9A-Fa-f]{2,8})',open(sys.argv[1]).read())))\" blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.uplus_dec` (also `\\U0001F600`, `%u`, `0x`). Names (`LATIN CAPITAL LETTER N`) -> `unicodedata.lookup` |\n| Only `+ - .` (or `+-<>[].,`), runs of one sign then `.`, ~31 B per char, no letters (prompt \"language\", \"small\", \"brain\", \"food for thought\", \"say\") | Brainfuck (D26), loop-free one-cell form: `python3 -c \"import sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read();print(''.join(chr((t[:i].count('+')-t[:i].count('-'))%256) for i,c in enumerate(t) if c=='.'))\" blob.txt`; anything with `<>[]` -> `lib.warmups_codecs.bf_dec`. NEVER run untrusted programs outside a step-capped pure-python interpreter |\n| `Ook. Ook. Ook! Ook! ...` -- 2-3 word+mark tokens, ~314 B per char (prompt quotes the tokens, \"chatty\", \"monkey\", \"ape\", \"librarian\"); `Blub.`, `MoO mOo`, `pi ka pikachu`, any <= 8 repeating words with run structure | BF token dialect (D27 Ook!): `python3 -I -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.bfd_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (Ook/Blub/COW/Pikalang/Alphuck/ReverseFuck/Binaryfuck + crib-solved unknown map). Hand: tokens `re.findall(r'Ook([.?!])')`, pair from 0, `Ook! Ook.` = OUTPUT. Sanity: number of output pairs == sentence length (~108-110) |\n| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\nThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n\n## Day by day\n**Days 1-13** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal, URL `%xx`, decimal entities, quoted\n`\"\\u00XX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, reversed sentence, Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s. Lessons (details in\nPitfalls): D1 same-named downloads overwrote each other (match by sha256); D3 a slow English scorer timed Mastery out; D4\nrot-N needs a margin over the unshifted text; D6 a shell `read` loop lost the unterminated last token; D7 decoding the\nWHOLE Mastery zip gave another set's slug (read the member); D8 a `str.replace` patch silently no-op'd; D11 strict len%4\nrefused unpadded b64; D12 rot-N \"decoded\" reversed text (`69-fove-n`); D13 free lines must be assigned JOINTLY.\n\n**Days 14-27** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex; rail fence 3/offset 0; hex of UTF-16LE\n+ `fffe`; hex of UTF-16BE + `feff`; gzip in base64; zlib `78da` in base64; Adobe Ascii85 `<~...~>`; netstrings\n`1:N,1:i,...`; `U+XXXX` code points; loop-free one-cell Brainfuck; the same program in Ook! pairs): single CORRECT in\n4.5-39 s, first submit each day. Lessons (details in Pitfalls): D14 rank 3-word slugs first; D15 ROT47 needs\n`register_free`; D16/D17 a carrier's 16-symbol output is a middle layer; D17 Mastery needed an early return; D18 lib's\n`rail_solve` existed but the carried solver never routed to it; D19's BOM-aware `_hex_utf16` carried D20 and D21's\nmagic-sniffing `_b64z` carried D22 unchanged (write the GENERIC rule on day N); D21 Mastery died at 60 s on an ad-hoc\nsolver; D23 and D25 Mastery all-NN-wrong from a pre-decoded line (fix: `register_free(name, day, fn)` on the RAW line);\nD24 a strict whole-text cursor parser sits first -- a structural codec's repeating unit (`\\d+:.,`) IS the cue. D26\n(`+ - .` soup; atlas had rated BF LOW): worker wrote a step-capped `_bf` (~27 s); Mastery died at 60 s because the\nruntime DAYS[26] encoder (`'+'*ord(c)+'.>'`) didn't reproduce the GM's DELTA bytes. D27 (\"Ook. Ook? Ook! Someone seems\nchatty.\"/\"Monkey Business\"): 33929 B, 3393 Ook pairs, only `+ - .` pairs, no `Ook?` despite the prompt; lib's prebuilt\n`bfd_dec` covered it, single ~33 s; the solver's DAYS[27] Ook encoder was absolute again (Mastery solve 34.9 s of 60).\nA parallel live worker swapped `Ook! Ook.` (output) / `Ook. Ook!` (input). A program blob means INTERPRET (pure python,\nstep cap); when lib has a prebuilt decoder for the family, call it first and register ITS encoder.\n\n**Day 28: Back in Sixteen** (\"Our sixteen-fingered friend is walking backwards.\" -- REVERSED HEX).\n- 220-byte `blob.txt`, one line, `[0-9a-e]` only, even length, no newline: D3's hex of the sentence, the STRING reversed\n  char by char (`...6e4` at the end = `4e` \"N\" read backwards). Pun: D3's own riddle (\"sixteen fingers\" = hex) +\n  \"walking backwards\" = D12 reversal; title \"Back in Sixteen\" (Back in Black) = back(wards) + sixteen. Triage again said\n  \"looks base64\" (hex is a b64 subset) and its idea list said only \"hex\" -- the second clue came from the prompt verb.\n- Command: `python3 -c \"import sys;print(bytes.fromhex(open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip()[::-1]).decode())\" blob.txt`.\n  Forward `fromhex` gives `#S\\xd2GVvveu...` and byte-reversed gives `\\xe4\\x966V\\x02w...` (nibble-swapped) -- neither is\n  a slug; reverse the CHARACTERS (or byte-reverse + nibble-swap).\n- Single: the harness race probe (prebuilt lib `decode_chunk` reversal + hex / tgt:free-over-strict) submitted CORRECT at\n  start+8 s, first try; slug as printed. TT 5.1 s, Mastery 138 x 28 9.3 s (solve 6.2 s), both first try. The carried\n  solver registered NO day-28 codec: line 28 was the LAST Mastery line, so `_positional`'s unknown-last-line path\n  (`decode_chunk`: reversal then hex) decoded it. Tomorrow line 28 is a MIDDLE line -- register it (Pitfalls).\n- Lesson: after a 2-day esolang run the lane went back to COMPOSING two early codecs, cued by quoting an old prompt;\n  expect more remixes (reversed octal/binary/base64, rot13 over base64, ...) -- undo the outer free layer, then peel.","numLines":50,"startLine":160,"totalLines":275}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01V2m59M14Me9ZnauKz7kaNY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
